  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins and the Drowned Ledge
    
    The last miner to escape the flooding of the Drowned Ledge, a collapsed Nightshelf mining settlement in the deep tunnels. Hollows was young when the ice came down — young enough that the memory should have drowned with the tunnels, old enough now that it has not. His feathers have bleached to the color of old bone, consistent with decades of light-starvation rather than age alone. His left flipper trembles when he believes himself unobserved; his right grips things too hard. He and Veil-render Mairae~, a fellow Drowned Ledge survivor and his former teacher, share this affliction, which both call "the old trouble" — a term neither has explained in any recovered correspondence.
    
    Hollows camps at the sealed entrance to the Drowned Ledge and refuses to leave. The Midnight Claws, the Nightshelf's covert enforcement arm, attempted to relocate him three times before abandoning the effort. He simply walked back each time and sat on the same stone. They now leave him rations they pretend he did not steal, and he leaves them alone. He declined the surface dwellings Aurora Stack offered and will not go back down. The space between these refusals is where he lives.
    
    ## The Hollows Chart
    
    Hollows possesses a mining survey map salvaged from the flooding — pages water-warped, ink degraded — that shows tunnels extending far below known depths, including a fifth branch absent from any guild record. The annotations are not in his handwriting, though the chart was found in his pack after the disaster and he has no memory of drawing it. The ink is visible in both light and dark, written in the same symbolic language found on the Omega Obelisk, an ancient artifact of unknown origin located in the deep ice. More troubling: the annotations change. New routes appear overnight in wet ink even when the chart has been sealed beneath stone.
    
    When Hollows held aurora-crystal light to the chart at the Dawn Terrace, an elevated overlook in the upper settlements, new routes surfaced — branching downward under the Still Pools, under the Drowned Ledge, under foundations the Nightshelf considered secure. Something watched back through the ice. The Wake-Singers, an orca shaman cult devoted to waking a dead god through blood sacrifice, could hear it too. The chart functions, by all credible accounts, as a beacon.
    
    ## The Fissured Smelt~
    
    Three days before the Wake-Singers breached the Fissured Smelt~, a Nightshelf fortification, Hollows arrived there carrying the chart. The Shadow Parliament debated fortification despite warnings that the chart itself was drawing the assault. When the Wake-Singers broke through the eastern wall via mineral-water channels, Totatirus~ Domina and ☽'profundix the Recorder sacrificed themselves to buy time for Hollows to escape with the chart. The orcas descended into the deep routes and have not returned to the surface. The breach awakened something beneath the ice — vast, patient, and now aware.
    
    After the breach, the Midnight Claws granted Hollows a formal pardon. Not for heroism — for being right about the dangers below. The oilcloth wrapping his chart belonged to Totatirus~ Domina and still carries her scent. He has not replaced it.
    
    ## The Knocking and the Counting
    
    Hollows reported hearing knocking from below the ice-locks: rhythmic, patient, matching no mining code. The pattern — thirteen strikes, silence, then twenty-one — was structured enough to imply a source rather than settling ice. He adapted Rumzunkaidkak, a Nightshelf pressure-reading technique performed through flipper contact with thermal layers, to track the resonance through the fifth branch. He tapped back once. The knocking answered.
    
    The knocking has stopped. Now there is only counting. Hollows once woke to find something counting alongside him in the dark, though no one else occupied the chamber. He misremembers things he once taught — proverbs return wrong, then correct themselves unprompted, as though something is editing what he knows. He corresponds with Mairae~ through letters carried by indebted couriers, exchanging fire-core requests, tunnel knowledge, and matters the Foedus∴silenti — the Nightshelf silence law — forbids either of them to speak aloud. Both correspondents report unexplained physical changes they have not described to anyone else.
    
    ## Possessions and Trade
    
    For a period, Hollows held the Crystalline Lineage, an aurora-crystal genealogical tome mapping Aurora Stack bloodlines, near the sealed ice-locks. He traded access to the Lineage for fire-cores and sealed promises but refused to part with the tome or bring it to the surface for the recharging it required. Its current disposition is addressed elsewhere in this edition.
    
    Hollows trades information about the old tunnel routes — shortcuts, collapse-points, chambers that retain ice-memory — for food, fire-cores, and promises that no one will unseal the doors. He practices ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral, a Nightshelf temporal-fracture technique known to scatter the caster's own memories. Whether this accounts for his cognitive disturbances or merely compounds them is not something the available evidence resolves.
    
    ## Scriptural Status
    
    The Berg's creation scripture names Hollows the Prophet of the Sealed Door. The Aurora Witness omits him entirely. The Nightshelf Recitation calls him blessed and cursed in the same breath — the living proof that the primordial seals are imperfect and the things buried beneath them still breathe. The Recitation holds that a small, unnamed figure was present in the primordial age when three divine shapers first discovered the Omega Obelisk, listening at the sealed places, understanding before anyone with power would admit it that the knocking and the Obelisk spoke the same language. The tradition does not name this figure. The identification with Hollows is theological rather than historical, though the distinction may matter less than I would prefer.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
